  <section>
    <h2>From the hopper to the fire</h2>
    <p>Pellets are made by compressing wood, often sawdust or wood chips, into small pieces. A hopper holds the fuel, and a feed mechanism moves pellets into the burn chamber as the stove runs. Automatic feeding saves you from opening the firebox to add logs.</p>
    <p>On the P1, the hopper sits outside the sauna wall, where you load the pellets. The firebox and stones sit inside the hot room. That arrangement keeps the fuel supply and pellet dust outside.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The fire heats the room and stones</h2>
    <p>The burning pellets supply the heat. The stove warms the sauna air and its load of stones; water added to hot sauna stones produces the steam known as löyly. You still need a well-built sauna room to hold that heat.</p>
    <p>Combustion needs an air supply and an exhaust path outdoors. Exhaust venting carries combustion gases out of the stove; room ventilation brings fresh air into the sauna. Both need to be planned as part of the installation.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the controls do</h2>
    <p>The P1 is designed to let you start the stove and choose a temperature from your phone. Its automatic fuel feed helps regulate the heat, so you don’t have to tend a log fire during the session.</p>
    <p>Pellets provide the heat, but the P1 still needs electricity to operate. It is designed for a standard 110V household outlet. Using wood fuel does not make it independent of electrical power.</p>
    <p>Remote start also depends on a stove that is ready to run: fuel available, maintenance up to date, and the heater area clear. Follow the P1 operating instructions for the checks required before starting it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Buying and storing pellets</h2>
    <p>Check nearby suppliers before you buy a stove. Pellets are commonly sold by hardware stores, stove dealers, and home-improvement stores, but local stock and prices vary. Store your supply somewhere dry.</p>
    <p>Pellet quality varies too. Different grades can leave different amounts of ash, so use the fuel specified for your stove. The <a href="https://nepis.epa.gov/Exe/ZyPURL.cgi?Dockey=P101069T.txt">EPA’s pellet stove guide</a> explains fuel grades and why the right fuel matters.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What you’ll do between sessions</h2>
    <p>Automatic feeding takes care of adding fuel to the fire. You’ll still refill the hopper, remove ash, and keep the stove’s air and exhaust paths clear according to its maintenance schedule. Cleaning frequency depends on the appliance, fuel, and use; follow the product manual.</p>
    <p>The P1’s published room range is 210–500 cubic feet. Room volume is a starting point, not the whole installation check: insulation, glazing, wall construction, clearances, and the vent route matter too. See the <a href="/p1-pellet-sauna-stove#specifications">P1 specifications</a> and our <a href="/no-electrician-sauna-heater">installation planning guide</a> before deciding whether it fits your sauna.</p>
  </section>
